[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Static Members and Classes in C#"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In C#, the 'static' keyword is used to declare members that belong to the type itself rather than to a specific instance of the type. This means that static members are shared across all instances of the class, and can be accessed directly using the class name."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Static Members"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Static members can be fields, methods, properties, events, or constructors. They are useful for representing data or behavior that is common to all instances of a class."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Static Fields"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"A static field is a variable that is shared by all instances of a class. It is declared using the 'static' keyword."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"public class MyClass {
 public static int count = 0;
 public MyClass() {
 count++;
 }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'count' is a static field that keeps track of the number of instances of 'MyClass' that have been created. Each time a new instance is created, the 'count' field is incremented. You can access the static field using the class name, like this: 'int numberOfInstances = MyClass.count;'"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Static Methods"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"A static method is a method that can be called directly on the class itself, without creating an instance of the class. It is declared using the 'static' keyword."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"public class MyMath {
 public static int Add(int a, int b) {
 return a + b;
 }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'Add' is a static method that adds two integers. You can call the static method using the class name, like this: 'int sum = MyMath.Add(5, 3);'"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Static Properties"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"A static property is similar to a static field, but it provides a way to control access to the underlying data. It is declared using the 'static' keyword."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"public class Configuration {
 private static string _connectionString;
 public static string ConnectionString {
 get { return _connectionString; }
 set { _connectionString = value; }
 }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'ConnectionString' is a static property that provides access to the '_connectionString' field. You can access the static property using the class name, like this: 'string connection = Configuration.ConnectionString;'"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Static Constructors"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"A static constructor is used to initialize static fields or properties of a class. It is called automatically before the first instance of the class is created or any static members are referenced. A static constructor cannot have parameters or access modifiers."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"public class MyClass {
 public static string message;
 static MyClass() {
 message = "Hello from static constructor!";
 }
}"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the static constructor initializes the 'message' static field. This constructor will run only once, before any instances of 'MyClass' are created or 'MyClass.message' is accessed."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Static Classes"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"A static class is a class that cannot be instantiated. It can only contain static members. Static classes are useful for grouping together related utility functions or constants."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"public static class StringHelper {
 public static bool IsNullOrEmpty(string str) {
 return string.IsNullOrEmpty(str);
 }
}"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'StringHelper' is a static class that contains a static method 'IsNullOrEmpty'. You can call the static method using the class name, like this: 'bool isEmpty = StringHelper.IsNullOrEmpty(myString);' You cannot create an instance of 'StringHelper'."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Static members belong to the class itself, not to instances of the class."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Static members are shared across all instances of the class."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Static members can be accessed directly using the class name."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Static classes cannot be instantiated."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Static classes can only contain static members."}]
